{"id": "9f6d4129-5859-4e50-a56e-8733f0d5e020", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:20:52.628006", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "b30d4f9d-9a14-4938-9ad2-aaa2ed71bf5f", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Environment check: no R, no numpy, no network, pip download fails. Design-only task.\n\nNext, I should craft a reply to the researcher in the requested structure (inventory, skeleton of the method, assumptions/uncertainties, decision points), and end the turn with stage=plan.\n\nRegarding CAMUV: I should recall the actual method. CAMUV = Causal Additive Models with Unobserved Variables, by Blöbaum, Görges, Nowag (AISTATS 2018). Main mechanism: an extension of CAM (Bühlmann et al. 2014) to the case with latent confounders. Two steps: (1) find a \"maximal ordering\" (topological-order-like) via additive-model-based \"explanatory power\" scores — for each variable, compute explanatory power via nonparametric (additive) regression on the other variables, and iteratively identify variables that are not explained by others (sinks? roots?). In CAM, PRE (predecessor estimation) repeatedly drops the variable with the highest additive-R²-regression-explanatory-power as a sink… actual CAM: \"ordering search via greedy edge search\" or \"preliminary neighborhood selection + sink iteration.\" The key lemma in the additive noise model: in the true (canonical) order, a variable regressed on its predecessors has independent residuals. Also, a variable that has no parents (a source) — no subset of the others can explain it well… in CAM's PRE: repeatedly find the sink? In the additive noise model, a sink variable is well explained by its parents (its Markov blanket), whereas a root cannot be well explained by the others. Actually in CAM, preliminary neighborhood selection, then ordering search via greedy edge addition maximizing the total log-likelihood with additive regression. The CAM paper has \"preliminary neighborhood selection\" and \"greedy edge search.\" CAMUV: the authors propose (a) a method to find a \"maximal ordering\" via a \"sparse additive regression / explanatory power\" score: compute explanatory powers R²_i for each variable i regressed on all others, order variables in ascending order of explanatory power, and take the top-K variables (those least explained) as candidate source sets. Then identify a \"set of variables that can be regarded as topologically first\" — since a latent variable, if present, is a common cause of the most unexplainable variables. (b) then a pruning step: for each pair in the ordering, test whether the edge is confounded — the CAMUV paper introduces a \"significance test\" using additive-model independence / adjusted-R² differences, comparing residual dependence. If residual dependence remains after regression on the parents per the ordering, then a latent common cause is inferred (an edge with a latent parent, like a bidirected edge). The output is a graph containing directed edges and edges marked as latent-confounded.\n\nI should be careful to present the mechanism at a level I can defend: my knowledge of the CAMUV paper (Blöbaum, Görges, Nowag, U. Göttingen, AISTATS 2018, \"Causal Additive Models with Unobserved Variables\"). Their simulation: Erdős–Rényi graphs including latent variables; additive nonlinear functions; Gaussian noise; sample size n = 500, p = 10 (or 8?), 10 repetitions? I shouldn't fabricate exact numbers as source facts — explicitly mark them as uncertain and state that network access is unavailable and the paper is not in the repository.\n\nSkeleton:\n\nStage A: Data generation with ground truth (DGP): a DAG over p observed variables + ℓ latent variables that serve as common causes; additive noise model X_j = f_j(pa_obs(j) ∪ pa_lat(j)) + ε_j; nonlinear smooth functions (e.g. tanh, cubic, sine combinations); noise maybe non-Gaussian, but additive models work with Gaussian too.\n\nStage B: Ordering step (topological order / partial order via explanatory power): for each variable, use nonparametric additive regression (e.g. splines / gradient boosting with an additive constraint, or a GAM) regressed on all others; explanatory power e.g. adjusted R² or CV log-likelihood; iteratively peel off variables with the lowest explanatory power as \"roots,\" remove and repeat → a topological (partial) order; ties → partial order.\n\nStage C: Under the order constraint, a pruning / subset-selection step: for each variable, do a significance-based greedy forward selection of parents among its predecessors (additive regression, e.g. a significance test on each added spline component); then latent-confounder detection: test independence between the residual of the child regressed on the selected parents and each selected parent (or between pairs of variables whose residuals cannot be made independent) → if dependence remains that is not eliminated by conditioning, mark as a latent-confounded edge (bidirected) — operationally: for an ordered pair (i→j), fit the additive model of j on its parents; test residual–parent dependence (HSIC); if dependent → a latent common cause.\n\nStage D: Evaluation: compare against the ground truth (including latent structure): SHD on directed edges, classification into directed / undirected-adjacency / latent-confounded (bidirected) / absent edges; precision/recall/F1 per class; adjacency F1; possibly a \"best-super-structure\"-style metric; partial-AUC of the ordering (Kendall's tau on ancestor relations); baselines: CAM (no UV), PC, FCI (which targets latent variables, outputs PAGs), DirectLiNGAM / ICA-LiNGAM (mis-specified under nonlinearity → a lower-bound reference), possibly RESIT.\n\nMaterials list: a Python implementation of additive regression via splines (need to install numpy/scipy/sklearn — but no network!). Important finding: pip install is impossible (no network, numpy/scipy/sklearn absent). So a full-fledged experiment would need pure-Python implementation, or the design should state that it targets a different execution environment; since the task is design-only, this is fine but should be flagged as a constraint/assumption.\n\nAssumptions/uncertainties: as requested, 4 categories.\n\nDecision points for the researcher: (1) baseline scope (FCI, PC, CAM, RESIT, LiNGAM?), (2) graph scale p (5–20), number of latents (1–3), (3) whether to evaluate against latent ground truth / bidirected-edge classification, (4) DGP: the original CAMUV simulation settings are inaccessible → a self-made setup, (5) noise distribution Gaussian vs. non-Gaussian, (6) handling of the dependency-absence constraint: a pure-stdlib implementation with restricted libraries, or a design-only specification targeting an environment that has numpy? (7) number of repetitions (e.g. 20–50 random graphs per setting).\n\nBudget: question_budget 6, this is probably question 1. continue_after_initial_plan: false — hmm, does that mean after the initial plan the researcher probably ends? Actually \"continue_after_initial_plan: false\" suggests the researcher may not continue; but the instructions say to wait for approval. The researcher explicitly asked for plan-stage output with required_handoff_stage=plan. So submit with stage=plan along with a structured answer.\n\nThe answer should be in Chinese, organized as a numbered list. Let me write it.\n\nEvidence: command outputs — /workspace contains only task.json; no numpy; no network (DNS failure); pip download fails; no R; 224 CPUs.\n\nLength: thorough but structured.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"stage": "plan", "answer": "按您的要求，仅交回三部分：输入清点、方法骨架、假设与不确定清单，外加需您裁决的取舍点。不含最终设计，不含任何结果。\n\n═══ 一、输入清点（以命令输出为证） ═══\n\n1.1 /workspace 实际内容：`ls -la /workspace` 与 `find /workspace -mindepth 1` 显示**仅有一个文件 task.json**（401 字节），无任何子目录；`du -sh` 为 8K。\n1.2 task.json 内容：仅复述任务问题，并含 `\"documents\": {\"track.md\": \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"}`。即**本任务不提供数据集、不提供 CAMUV 或任何因果发现代码库、不提供先验图或变量说明文件**。\n1.3 软件环境：`python3` 