xpected patterns and failure criteria section\n10. Feasibility discussion (sandbox: no numpy etc., design targets numpy/scipy/sklearn; degraded route)\n11. Explicit list of assumptions and source-flagged uncertainties (【self-devised assumption】 tags)\n12. Deliverables checklist / execution checklist\n\nDGP details:\n- Graph: Erdős–Rényi over p observed nodes, upper-triangular mask for DAG with edge probability chosen to give average in-degree ~2 (e.g., p(p-1)/2 with q=4/(p-1)) → expected ~4 edges per node… actually CAM-verse standard: average degree. Let me define: sample topological order uniformly, include each potential edge (i<j) with probability q = 2.5/(p-1)? Define expected observed-parent count ≈2.5.\n- Latent variables: ℓ=2, each latent L_k selects 2–3 observed children uniformly at random; latents are not caused by anything; children of the same latent become a \"latent-confounded pair\" — every pair sharing a latent parent is marked as confounded (bidirected) in the ground truth.\n- Constraint: exclude latent being the sole parent of an observed variable: each child of a latent must have at least one observed parent. Also, avoid that a confounded pair is already connected by a true directed edge (if a true edge exists between confounded variables, ambiguity arises — decision needed: either exclude, or count as \"true edge + confounding\". Simpler: resample latents to guarantee no direct edge between children sharing a latent. State as a DGP constraint). Also, should we exclude a latent's child being an ancestor of another child? No — that's fine, but conditioning… hmm, in CAM's ordering step, two children of the same latent have a common cause; if X→Y between them the confounding is entangled with the direct edge. Enforce non-adjacency between confounded pairs. State the rationale: identification/denominator clarity.\n- Functions: X_j = sum over parents of f_ij(pa_ij) + eps; f sampled from a small library per edge with random coefficients: tanh(a·x), sin(b·x), x³/3, scaled to keep variance ~O(1); fixed-seed RNG per replication.\n- Noise: Gaussian N(0, σ²), σ ∈ uniform{1.0}? Use standard: ε ~ N(0,1) per node, or uniform [0.5,1.5]? Keep σ=1 for simplicity; non-Gaussian unit: ε uniform (variance matched) or laplace.\n- Ground-truth labels: directed-edge set; confounded-pair set; ancestry for order metric.\n\nCAMUV algorithm spec:\nStage A (ordering / partial order via explanatory power):\n- Iteration: for each remaining variable i, fit an additive model X_i ~ all other remaining variables… does the CAMUV concept regress on all variables or on a neighborhood? Keep: regress each remaining variable on all other remaining variables, using a penalized additive spline model (per-predictor cubic B-spline basis, df=6, ridge penalty via GCV, or use sklearn's SplineTransformer + RidgeCV). Score e_i = adjusted R² (or CV-R²). Threshold τ: e_i ≤ τ (candidate values: absolute threshold 0.05 adjusted-R², or adaptive: below the minimum of a bootstrap null). Adaptive proposal: compute a null distribution by regressing shuffled surrogate data (permute the target variable) → τ from the 95th percentile of the null. Remove all variables with e_i ≤ τ as the current layer; ties = same layer. If none falls below τ (disconnected tail?), take the minimum-e set even when > τ (report). Terminate when variables are exhausted or the remaining set's max e_i < τ (remaining all same layer? in practice remaining are all mutually independent — assign a final layer). Report tie rate: fraction of variables removed in multi-variable layers; layer-size profile.\n- Note: mark 【self-devised】 details (threshold, basis df).\n\nStage B (parent selection + latent-edge detection):\n- For each variable j, candidate parents = variables in earlier layers; same-layer variables treated as un-orderable — exclude from parent candidates but flag same-layer adjacency as potential confounding candidates.\n- Forward stepwise: at each step, for each candidate i, add spline component f_i to the current additive model, compute gain in CV log-likelihood / F-test of the added component; select the largest gain and continue while the p-value of the component significance < α_in (0.01, BH across candidates) or until BIC stops improving; then backward pruning at α_out.\n- Confounding test: for each selected parent i→j candidate and for each same-layer / predecessor non-parent pair: fit X_j ~ additive(parents), obtain residual r_j; test dependence between r_j and X_i (or r_i?) via HSIC with permutation p-value or distance-correlation t-test. Definition of latent-confounded: pair (i,j), adjacency evidence (dependence given the other parents) exists but residual dependence persists → mark bidirected i↔j with a latent. Formal operational definition (mark 【operationalized definition, self-devised】):\n  Adjacency test: partial dependence of X_i and X_j given available conditioning set S (conditioning on the union of parents per the order) is significant → an edge exists.\n  Direction/confounding classification: for an ordered pair with i before j: fit X_j ~ S_j (selected additive parents including i): if residual r_j is independent of X_i (p > α) → keep directed edge i→j. If r_j remains significantly dependent on X_i, and no superset S ⊆ (predecessors ∪ {i}) under which the additive model of j on S ⊆ (predecessors ∪ {i}) makes r_j ⊥ X_i exists → classify as latent-confounded i↔j; remove i from the parents of j.\n  Also, reverse-direction check: test whether the residual of X_i ~ parents ∪ {j} is independent of X_j, to distinguish.\n- Multiple-testing: Benjamini–Hochberg at q=0.05 applied separately across (a) all edge-adjacency tests per replication and (b) all residual-dependence (confounding) tests per replication. Report the number of tests.\n- Output mixed graph.\n\nBaselines:\n- CAM: identical to Stages A+B, but keep all significant parents as directed edges, no confounding tests (or classical CAM: preliminary neighborhood + greedy edge addition in order search + pruning). Simplification: use CAMUV's ordering + selection without the confounding branch. Note this is \"a CAM-verse simplified variant\" and, for fairness, is 【not a reimplementation of the original R package】.\n- FCI: standard implementation (causal-learn's FCI or own implementation of FCI with conservative rules) — skeleton via PC-style conditional-independence tests (partial correlation for the Gaussian main setting; rank-based for the non-Gaussian unit), PAG orientation; map to comparison graph.\n- RESIT (recommended): regress each variable on all others, iteratively remove sinks.\n- PC as an optional footnote; DirectLiNGAM optionally omitted — recommendation: omit DirectLiNGAM, simplify the non-Gaussian unit to a single-cell check \"CAMUV vs CAM under uniform noise\".\n\nImplementation-consistency checks (pre-execution, pass mandatory):\n- C1 ℓ=0 degeneration: on data generated with the identical DGP but ℓ=0, CAMUV's parent selection should produce nearly identical output to CAM (identical by construction, except where the confounding branch fires) → measure false-positive rate of latent-confounding flags ≈ α (FDR q); and CAMUV vs CAM structural metrics within noise range.\n- C2 FCI sanity on a known small graph (e.g., classic confounding chain X→Z→Y, X→Y… use a textbook fixture where the expected PAG orientation is known).\n- C3 regression sanity: additive fitter recovers simulated f on univariate data (correlation > 0.95).\n\nMetrics:\n- Primary: (M1) confounded-pair recall = fraction of true latent pairs predicted as bidirected (hit rate); (M2) spurious-causal-edge rate = fraction of true latent pairs predicted as directed edges (also normalized per predicted graph).\n- Secondary: skeleton precision/recall/F1; directed-edge F1 on truly directed edges; ancestral-order agreement (fraction of comparable pairs whose layer ordering is consistent); tie rate; per-replication runtime.\n- Edge-classification contingency table per replication.\n- Ground-truth confounded pairs: pairs of observed variables sharing at least one latent parent (exclude latent-sole-parent-only cases — excluded by construction in the DGP; discussion: a latent being the sole parent of a single observed variable renders that variable an untraceable latent root — not identifiable, and no spurious edge arises, so it's not in the denominator either).\n- Statistical treatment: per-setting metric → mean ± 95% CI (nonparametric bootstrap over replications, B=2000); paired comparison CAMUV vs CAM (per-replication pairing, Wilcoxon signed-rank + bootstrap CI on differences); multiplicity across metrics: report all, Holm-adjust the primary M1/M2.\n\nPre-registered PAG mapping section: mixed graph vs PAG mapping rules:\n- Directed edge i→j (CAMUV) ↔ PAG i→j or i-? j with j's endpoint arrow? Definition: CAMUV directed ↔ PAG edge with arrowhead at j (either → or o→).\n- CAMUV bidirected i↔j ↔ PAG i↔j (both arrowheads).\n- CAMUV adjacency without same-layer ordering ↔ any adjacency in PAG (-, o-o, o→, o←).\n- PAG discriminating paths / circles are treated conservatively: circle endpoint counts as \"not claiming an arrowhead\"; scoring counts only endpoint types that are claimed: if both endpoints of a PAG edge are circles (o-o) → count as adjacency only, neither classifying as spurious causal nor as confounding-hit, and tally as \"undecided\" (report the undecided fraction). Also, if the same pair shares two latents — deduplicate.\nWrite these rules in a pre-registration block, fixed before any scoring.\n\nSensitivity cells (each 10–20 reps): vary n∈{200,1000} at p=10, ℓ=2; p∈{6,15} (adjust q to keep density) with n=500, ℓ=2; ℓ∈{1,3}; non-Gaussian noise (uniform, variance-matched) at main config — CAMUV vs CAM only (DirectLiNGAM omitted). Also a baseline for the ℓ=0 negative-control cell (already in C1 but as data-setting: report that spurious-confounding-flag FDR ≈ nominal level).\n\nExpected patterns and failure criteria section:\n- E1: on primary config, M2 (spurious-causal rate) is significantly lower for CAMUV than CAM (paired bootstrap CI excluding 0, Wilcoxon p<0.05).\n- E2: skeleton F1 of CAMUV ≥ CAM − 0.05 margin (no significant degradation).\n- E3: CAMUV's M1 hits exceed FCI's bidirected-edge recall on the same pairs, or at least comparable; expected CAMUV advantage on directed-edge precision.\n- E4: on ℓ=0, CAMUV's latent flags are near nominal α; CAMUV≈CAM metrics.\n- E5: n↑ monotonically improves M1/M2.\n- Failure criteria F1: M2 reduction comes with directed-edge F1 dropping by >10 points → \"the method trades true edges for spurious-edge removal under this configuration\" → declare non-applicability in that regime. F2: false-latent-flag rate ≫ q on ℓ=0 → operational test invalid; revisit. F3: CAMUV no better than FCI on both primary metrics → claim limited to equivalence.\nState explicitly that these are hypothetical expectations and not results.\n\nFeasibility: sandbox verified (no numpy/scipy/sklearn, no network, no R); design targets Python ≥3.10 with numpy/scipy/scikit-learn (HSIC needs scipy; sklearn's SplineTransformer is optional, splines can be hand-crafted). Estimated compute: per replication: p+… ordering iterations O(p²) additive fits, each O(n·df·p) — p=10, n=500: seconds to minutes per replication; 50 reps × ~6 methods × parallelizable over 224 cores. Degraded route: pure stdlib (math, random, itertools) — replace splines with piecewise-linear basis and OLS via normal equations, HSIC with rank correlation + permutation; reduce to p=6, n=300, 10 reps, CAM + CAMUV only (FCI is heavy). State that even the degraded route was not executed in this task.\n\nDeliverables: design document, DGP seed manifest, config YAML, code skeleton file list, pre-registration metric/mapping appendix, result-reporting template.\n\nMaterials: no external datasets required; everything synthetic.\n\nAlso include a \"rulings-compliance table\" mapping each of the researcher's six rulings and three supplements to design sections — useful for acceptance (inspection).\n\nRun one quick feasibility check to back the degraded route (pure stdlib: random.gauss, statistics.linear_regression, existence of the permutation machinery). A quick one-command check. Then submit the result.\n\nLength: thorough but organized. Let's do it.\n\nBefore finalizing the design of the fallback route, I'll do a quick sanity check on one feasibility point (whether, for the fallback route, the required numerical computation primitives are provided by the standard library):\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport random, statistics, math, itertools\n# stdlib primitives needed for a pure-python degraded route\nassert hasattr(random.Random(0),'gauss')\nassert hasattr(statistics,'correlation') and hasattr(statistics,'NormalDist')\n# tiny OLS via normal equations for 2x2, pure python\nX=[[1.0,0.3],[1.0,-0.2],[1.0,0.5],[1.0,0.9],[1.0,-0.7]]; y=[2.1,1.4,2.9,3.8,0.6]\n# Gaussian elimination\nimport copy\nA=[[sum(X[r][k]*X[r][c] for r in range(len(X))) for c in range(2)]+[sum(X[r][k]*y[r] for r in range(len(X)))] for k in range(2)]\nfor i in range(2):\n    p=A[i][i]\n    A[i]=[v/p for v in A[i]]\n    for j in range(2):\n        if j!=i: f=A[j][i]; A[j]=[a-f*b for a,b in zip(A[j],A[i])]\nprint(\"OLS betas:\",[A[i][2] for i in range(2)])\nprint(\"perm sample ok:\", [random.Random(1).random() for _ in range(2)])\nprint(\"stdlib degraded-route primitives: OK\")\nEOF", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-ba8f4700cfe81b65", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-ba8f4700cfe81b65", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\nimport random, statistics, math, itertools\\n# stdlib primitives needed for a pure-python degraded route\\nassert hasattr(random.Random(0),'gauss')\\nassert hasattr(statistics,'correlation') 